WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:06.880
Colombia is marking one month since a wave&nbsp;
of deadly protests began. They started as an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:13.520
outcry against a controversial tax reform but soon&nbsp;
turned into a national uprising against growing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:20.080
poverty and inequality. Weeks of negotiations&nbsp;
to end the unrest have failed to bear fruit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:26.000
And the prospect of a peaceful solution seemed to&nbsp;
slip even further on Friday, when President Ivan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:31.600
Duque announced he was deploying the military&nbsp;
after more people died in demonstrations.&nbsp;

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:38.080
Plumes of smoke tower above the city of&nbsp;
Popayan - like monuments to the resistance.&nbsp;

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:44.720
A month of protests has turned streets across the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:48.800
country into smoldering battle-scars from&nbsp;
what they see as their fight for justice.&nbsp;

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:57.360
"The message we want to deliver is that we are&nbsp;
tired of so much injustice from this corrupt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.360 --> 00:01:02.880
government, of so much inequality.&nbsp;
We are not asking for any handouts.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:08.560
We are not asking them to give us anything.&nbsp;
We're only asking for an opportunity to grow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:13.680
for equality - not only for the&nbsp;
rich, but also for the poor."&nbsp;

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:25.840
The protests erupted when President Ivan Duque&nbsp;
sought to raise taxes to pay for the pandemic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:31.520
The blowback forced him to kill the bill.&nbsp;
But the outcry continued - in outrage over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:40.000
the government's handling of a crisis that's&nbsp;
seen unemployment double and inequality surge.&nbsp;

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:43.440
Attempts to broker an end to the&nbsp;
standoff have so far proven futile.&nbsp;

00:01:45.280 --> 00:01:51.120
According to official figures, more than 45 people&nbsp;
have been killed, dozens more disappeared and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:57.440
thousands on both sides wounded. But human rights&nbsp;
groups say the real toll is likely much higher.&nbsp;

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:01.200
And it's only likely to get&nbsp;
worse in the days ahead.&nbsp;

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:08.400
On Friday, President Duque said he's&nbsp;
deploying the army to epicenter of the unrest:&nbsp;

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:13.280
Cali, where at least three people&nbsp;
were killed in the last 24 hours.&nbsp;

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:25.840
"Let me be absolutely clear: As of tonight,&nbsp;
we begin the maximum deployment of military&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:29.760
assistance in the city of Cali&nbsp;
and (the department of) El Valle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:35.840
Islands of anarchy do not&nbsp;
have a place in our country."&nbsp;

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:45.680
Meeting with Colombia's Vice President on Friday,&nbsp;
US Secretary of State Antony Blinken expressed his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:51.520
concern over the deepening crisis in the country -&nbsp;
backing the peoples' right to protest peacefully.&nbsp;

00:02:53.520 --> 00:03:03.840
For now, though, the deadly cat-and-mouse&nbsp;
game on the streets of Colombia continues.

